    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Odyssey grew out of an oral tradition over hundreds of years, and whether <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> lived, and when, is still debated.</li><li>Its story of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Odysseus</a>'s return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ithaca">Ithaca</a> turns on homecoming, wandering, guest-friendship, testing and omens, and gives women and slaves larger roles than most ancient literature.</li><li>It was a school text across the Greek and Roman worlds and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, and its retellings run from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> and Joyce to modern novels and films.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Penelope tests the returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Odysseus</a> by asking to have their bed moved; only he knows it cannot be, because he carved it from the trunk of a living olive tree.</p></aside>
    </section>

