    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">8th or 7th century BC</span><span class="rf-text">The Odyssey is composed in Homeric Greek as part of a long oral tradition</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">6th century BC</span><span class="rf-text">The Homeric poems gain a canonical place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, probably performed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panathenaea">Panathenaia</a> festival</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2nd century BC</span><span class="rf-text">Alexandrian scholars such as Aristarchus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samothrace">Samothrace</a> study and edit the text</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1354</span><span class="rf-text">Nicholas Sigeros provides <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> and the Odyssey</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1488</span><span class="rf-text">Demetrios Chalkokondyles produces the first printed edition, printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1616</span><span class="rf-text">George Chapman's translations of both epics are published together, the first complete Odyssey in English</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1922</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-joyce">James Joyce</a> publishes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Ulysses</a>, a reworking of the Odyssey set in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Odyssey is one of the two major epics of ancient Greek literature attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>. It was first composed in Homeric Greek around the 8th or 7th century BC, though there is no consensus on its date. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> began adapting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenician-alphabet">Phoenician alphabet</a> during the eighth century BC, and if the Homeric poems were among the earliest products of that literacy, they would have been composed late in that century. The poems' language, however, can be dated to long before widespread literacy.</p><p>Scholars agree that the Homeric epics developed within an oral tradition over hundreds of years. Both poems assume that their audiences already knew the story of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a>, which indicates that they drew on an existing body of myth. The Odyssey does not directly refer to the events the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> depicts, and the two are generally thought to have formed independently; it is not clear which came first.</p><p>Scholars also note strong influences from Near Eastern myth and literature. Martin West pointed to parallels with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh">Epic of Gilgamesh</a>: both heroes travel to the ends of the earth and receive directions to the land of the dead from a divine woman who lives by the sea, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circe">Circe</a> in one poem and Siduri in the other.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Ten years after the Achaeans won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Odysseus</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ithaca">Ithaca</a>, has still not returned home. In his absence 108 suitors court his wife Penelope, who delays them by weaving a shroud for his father Laertes and secretly unweaving it each night. Guided by the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>, his son Telemachus travels to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nestor">Nestor</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelaus">Menelaus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> for news. Released from the nymph Calypso, Odysseus reaches the Phaeacians and tells them of his wanderings: the lotus-eaters, the cyclops Polyphemus, the bag of winds, the sorceress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circe">Circe</a>, the visit to the dead, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirens">Sirens</a>, Scylla and Charybdis, and the killing of the sun god's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, after which all his men were lost.</p><p>Back on Ithaca, disguised as a beggar, he tests the loyalty of his household, wins the archery contest with his own bow and kills the suitors; Telemachus hangs a group of female slaves who had been sexually used by them. Penelope tests her husband with the secret of their bed before they are reunited, and Athena ends the feud with the suitors' families.</p><p>The poem opens in medias res and is told largely through speech. Its central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">themes</a> include nostos (homecoming), wandering, xenia (guest-friendship), testing and omens.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the early twentieth century Milman Parry and Albert Lord showed that the Homeric poems have the marks of oral poetry, which allowed even an illiterate poet to improvise long poems through formulaic language. Their fieldwork on the South Slavic epic tradition decisively showed that the texts formed as oral poetry. Scholars still disagree on how the poems emerged from that tradition, and on whether <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> lived at all; since the nineteenth century these questions have been known as the Homeric Question.</p><p>The poem is composed in dactylic hexameter, a metre of six feet, with lines of twelve to seventeen syllables that usually form a complete sentence. Its language is simple, direct and fast-paced, but literary: its vocabulary was probably never the speech of any Greek population. Long similes drawn from nature and daily life, and fixed epithets, are characteristic of it.</p><p>Because the poem was performed for audiences by an aoidos or rhapsode, its content may have varied from telling to telling. The division into 24 books was probably made long after its composition, possibly by Alexandrian scholars or rhapsodes. Some scholars regard the Telemachy, books 1 to 4, as a later addition, and the true ending has been debated since antiquity.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> was celebrated in Greek society as a gifted and instructive poet. By the sixth century BC the Homeric poems had a canonical place in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, where the tyrant Peisistratos or his son Hipparchus founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panathenaea">Panathenaia</a>, a festival that probably featured performances of them. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> and the Odyssey were school texts wherever Greek was spoken, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> absorbed Homer into their own culture.</p><p>Scholars at the libraries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> and Pergamum studied and edited the poems, among them Zenodotus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristophanes-of-byzantium">Aristophanes of Byzantium</a> and Aristarchus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samothrace">Samothrace</a>. Allegorical readings were popular and were used to defend Homer against charges, made by the philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophanes">Xenophanes</a>, that he disrespected the gods; Porphyry read the cave of the nymphs as an image of human life.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> the epics remained school texts, and in the middle Byzantine period adults began to read them for pleasure. John Tzetzes wrote Homeric Allegories for the consort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel I Komnenos</a>, and Eustathios of Thessalonike wrote commentaries later generations treated as authoritative. In the quarrel of the Ancients and Moderns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, Anne Dacier defended Homer against Charles Perrault and Antoine Houdar de la Motte.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The poem came down through many ancient editions. Material derived from the first-century BC scholar Didymus divides them into city editions, known for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeolis">Aeolis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and Marseille, and individual editions prepared by scholars. The spread of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-language">Greek language</a> into the Byzantine era kept the text known; the scholar Armstrong suggests that without it the epics might have been forgotten, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beowulf">Beowulf</a> was.</p><p>In 1354 Nicholas Sigeros gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> and the Odyssey, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-boccaccio">Giovanni Boccaccio</a> persuaded the monk Leontius Pilatus to translate them into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> prose; he finished the Iliad and came close to finishing the Odyssey. The first printed edition was produced in 1488 by Demetrios Chalkokondyles, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and educated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> by Antonios Damilas.</p><p>Translations followed in the sixteenth century: Andreas Divus's word-for-word Latin, Girolamo Baccelli's Italian of 1582 and Salomon Certon's French of 1604. George Chapman's English Odyssey, published with his Iliad in 1616, was the first modern translation to enjoy wide success. Later versions include Dacier's French prose of 1716, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-pope">Alexander Pope</a>'s of the 1720s, produced with Elijah Fenton and William Broome, and Vasily Zhukovsky's Russian of 1849.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Odyssey and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a> formed the basis of education in the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, a curriculum later taken up by Western humanists, and they mark the beginning of the Western literary tradition. Edith Hall notes that the Odyssey has been regarded as the birthplace of literary fiction, and in 2018 a BBC Culture poll of experts named it literature's most enduring narrative.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Odysseus</a> in the eighth circle of hell and gave him a new ending in which he never returns home. Some of his adventures reappear in the Arabic tales of Sinbad the Sailor. George Chapman's translation inspired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-keats">John Keats</a>'s sonnet of 1816, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-joyce">James Joyce</a>'s Ulysses of 1922 reworked the poem in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> of his own day; Joyce's use of the myth in turn inspired Joseph Campbell's idea of the hero's journey, which most scholars today consider reductive. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-atwood">Margaret Atwood</a>'s The Penelopiad and Madeline Miller's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/circe">Circe</a> retell it from the side of its women.</p><p>Monteverdi's opera Il ritorno d'Ulisse in patria (1640) and Fauré's Pénélope (1913) set its second half to music, and films range from L'Odissea of 1911 to O Brother, Where Art Thou? The psychiatrist Jonathan Shay related the poem to the homecoming of combat veterans.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Odyssey, attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> and composed around the 8th or 7th century BC out of a long oral tradition, tells of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Odysseus</a>'s ten-year journey home from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> and his return to Penelope. Read in Greek and Roman schools and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, printed in 1488 and translated widely, it has shaped Western literature from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> to Joyce.</em></p>

